Playing chess against the computer got harder.

Playing chess against a human opponent, which
is what most people do, is much the same as
it was, of course -- unless your human opponent
changes.

The other thing is, computer chess generally
uses the wonky "international" rules, which
appear to have been specifically designed to
make the game easier for computers.  Notably,
you can't go more than a certain number of
moves, twenty IIRC, without a pawn move or
a capture, or it's a stalemate.  I've never met
anybody who plays with this rule when there
are no computers involved, and I've never
seen a chess set that had this rule in its
rulebook.  The only conceivable reason for
this rule is to limit the total number of moves
in the game so that all possible games can
be exhaustively cataloged in a database
and analyzed statistically by a computer.
Under the normal rules, there are a finite
number of *positions* in chess but not a
finite number of possible games.  This may
seem like a subtle distinction, but it makes
a large difference in the difficulty of writing
software to statistically analyze all possible
outcomes of a given move and assign
probabilities for that move leading to a win,
a loss, or a draw.


